    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Films With Impossible Moral Dilemmas\nauthor: Guest User\ndate: 2001-01-01\n---\nFilms With Impossible Moral Dilemmas\n\nA good sign of solid characters in film is when they are forced to make difficult decisions, forcing the audience to be invested in what choices they'll make. This will either bug an audience if they feel like they would've done something completely different, or put them at ease if they feel like they've made the right choice. In some films, however, characters can be faced with impossible decisions that will have drastic consequences no matter how they go about it. In cases like this, we'll often leave the cinema wracking our minds, trying to figure out what would have been the right choice, if it was made, and if there even was one.\n\nPrisoners (2013)\n\nAfter his daughter is kidnapped, Keller Dover (Hugh Jackman) decides to try and find her himself since the police are taking their time. The moral dilemma comes in when the police stop investigating a suspect, who Keller Dover feels certain is guilty. After kidnapping the suspect, Keller is forced to decide whether he should torture a man who likely knows where his daughter is, or if he should refrain and let him go since there is a chance he's innocent. If Keller where to let him go, he'd be faced with the risk of letting a little girl die, and if he were to torture him he'd faced with the risk of severly harming someone innocent. Keller ends up torturing him, but unfortunately for his conscience the suspect turns out to be innocent. The moral dilemma shows how it's ineffective to inflict harm for the greater good, since the future is unpredictable despite our best guesses.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Watch Prisoners on Amazon Prime\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Buy Prisoners as DVD on Amazon\n\nThe Killing Of A Sacred Deer (2017)\n\nThe Killing Of A Sacred Deer is an adaptation of the Greek myth of Iphigenia In Aulisa. In the myth, King Agamemmon kills a deer, an animal sacred to the goddess Artemis, forcing him to sacrifice his daughter in order to make things right. In The film, a doctor's children start to become incredibly ill, and the son of a patient that died under the doctor's operation warns him that he must kill one member of his family, or else they'll all die. Director Yorgos Lanthimos gave the myth a medical twist and delivered a thrilling moral dilemma. The film uses dead"
