    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Rediscovering Literary Treasures: The Guestbooks of Village Writers Meet Their Neighbors, 1987\u20131996\ndate: 2025-02-03\n---\nRediscovering Literary Treasures: The Guestbooks of Village Writers Meet Their Neighbors, 1987\u20131996\n\nSince 2017, I have had the privilege of serving at the Hudson Park Library, a cornerstone of Greenwich Village's literary and artistic community since the 19th century. In my role as a librarian overseeing Adult Programming, I have organized an array of author talks, poetry readings, and gallery exhibitions. Yet one recent discovery has unearthed a forgotten chapter of our library's rich cultural history: the guestbooks of Village Writers Meet Their Neighbors, a literary program series held here between 1987 and 1996.\n\nVolumes of the Village Writers Meet Their Neighbors guestbook\n\nThese guestbooks transcend mere record-keeping; they are dynamic archives of a transformative period in Greenwich Village's literary landscape. They preserve not only the signatures of attendees but also a treasure trove of flyers, clippings, and writers' handwritten notes, offering a vivid glimpse into the era's cultural vibrancy. Within their pages, the names of literary luminaries such as Maurice Sendak, Grace Paley, and Vivian Gornick appear alongside emerging voices like James Lasdun and Sigrid Nunez. Together, these records evoke the spirit of a community where neighbors and authors converge, fostering connections through the shared power of storytelling and ideas.\n\nIn a 1987 article for The Villager, Michael Tomasky described the series as a celebration of local literary talent and community engagement. At its helm was Muriel Mandell, a prolific author of more than a dozen children's books, translated into over 12 languages, who chaired the Hudson Park Library Support Group. Mandell's vision was to highlight the literary vibrancy of Greenwich Village by uniting its residents with its talented writers. As she expressed, \"The neighborhood is a very exciting place, and we decided we'd tap some local talent and bring in some of the very talented people who live in the Village to the other wonderful people who live in the neighborhood.\"\n\nHighlights From The First Volume: 1987\u20131990\n\nThe series launched in January 1987 with Irini Spanidou, who discussed her debut novel, God's Snake. Other notable speakers followed, including Catharine Stimpson, who delved into themes of gender and literature in her talk,"
